  "evaluation": "Under the strict rules, `apparent` is limited to two cases: an underpowered null study or two findings that do not measure the same thing (e.g., a single mood item vs a full depression scale). Here, both studies measure depression cases (comorbid depression vs PHQ-9 > 5), and neither is an underpowered null, so `genuine` is the required verdict given the opposing `Found` directions. Response A misuses `apparent` to flag an unstated direction of effect and assigns `outcome_measure` even though both measure clinical depression/depressive risk. Responses B and C both correctly choose `genuine` with primary reason `population` (Alzheimer's disease patients vs hospitalized COVID-19 survivors) and `also_differs` of `outcome_measure` and `design_or_bias`. Response C is slightly better than B because it notes the ambiguity in Study B's direction within `detail` and `rationale` while still strictly adhering to the verdict rules.",
